<!--
    decbench F2: the error(nonzero,...)-conditional no-return recognizer
    (`option noreturn_error on|off`, default-ON, DIV-16).

    kuna analog of the value-conditional slice of Ghidra's discovered-no-return
    analyzer (and angr's CFGBase._determine_function_returning callgraph
    returning-fixpoint). glibc error(int status, int errnum, const char *fmt, ...)
    and error_at_line() call exit(status) and NEVER return WHEN status != 0 - but
    DO return for status == 0 - so `error` cannot be a Known no-return. An internal
    wrapper whose tail is `call error(2,...)` (GNU diffutils pfatal_with_name, and
    every die()-via-error helper) is nonetheless no-return; `noreturn_error` folds
    this recognition into the `noreturn_propagate` consumer (arg0 = the x86-64
    SysV `int status` register EDI = a nonzero literal), concludes the wrapper
    no-return, and its callers drop the dead fall-through.

    Like the whole no-return propagation family (`noreturn_propagate` /
    `noreturn_disc` / the Listing/xref tier, docs/listing-tier-design.md), the
    recognizer runs ONLY on the real-ELF bootstrap path - it reads the program-wide
    Listing, which is built in the console bootstrap, never on this XML
    `<binaryimage>` datatest path. So on the XML path the flag is INERT (there is
    no Listing to consume). The end-to-end behavior - an error(2,...) wrapper
    concluded no-return, its caller truncated, and an error(0,...) wrapper left
    returning - is proven by the cross-crate gate
    `decompiler/crates/kuna-console/tests/verify_noreturn_error.rs` against a real
    x86-64 fixture (`noreturn_error_x86_64`), the same way `noreturn_propagate` is
    proven by `verify_noreturn_propagate.rs`.

    This datatest proves the P0 assertion is WIRED:
      * `option noreturn_error on` / `off` PARSE and round-trip (an unknown option
        would make the console error and the second decompile would differ / not
        run), and
      * flipping it changes NOTHING about the rendered C on the XML path
        (default-inert parity): the same output appears under the default pipeline
        AND with `noreturn_error off`.

    Reuses the GH-558 bytes as a small, deterministic loadable function.

    Source:           int gh558(int x) { if (x LE 8) return 1; return 0; }
    Compiled (as):    cmp $8,%edi ; jg +6 ; mov $1,%eax ; ret ; xor %eax,%eax ; ret
-->
